ively Black: G8 vs White: H8, and now White to move.\nWhite has the advantage locally because Black wasted a stone at A1.\nI will pick **I9** as it seems like a great expanding move that creates threats.  Standard shape (Diagonal).\nI'm thinking aggressively.\nMy best aggressive moves (attached to H8) are **I9,** **H9** and **H7**.  I'm going to choose **I9**.\n\n**Final Review and Justification**\n\nI9 is a good expanding move because it creates a live diagonal, it opens up the board towards the larger empty space, and it doesn't immediately put me in contact with the opponent.\n\nThere are also H7 and H9. Both are good vertically. The move expands the influence into the space to the right/bottom. This seems like a better path. I'll stick with **I9.**\n\n**Move Selection**\nThe move is **I9**. I've checked and double-checked the coordinates.\nI'm confident in my selection of **I9**.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation:\nThe current board shows an opening phase where Black (X) has occupied A1 and G8, while White (O) occupies H8. Black\'s move at A1 is far from the center of action and effectively acts as a passed turn or a handicap, giving White a significant positional advantage in the center. The core battle is between Black\'s G8 and White\'s H8. White should aim to develop a strong shape that creates potential lines (2-in-a-row) which are "live" (open on both ends) and flexible for future attacks.\n\nTop candidate moves for White:\n1.  **I9**: This move creates a diagonal connection with H8. It forms a live 2 (diagonal) that points towards the large open space in the bottom-right. It maintains a good distance from Black\'s G8, avoiding immediate blocking battles while maintaining the initiative.\n2.  **H9** (or H7): This forms a vertical live 2 with H8. It is a solid move but slightly more rigid than the diagonal extension.\n3.  **G9**: This forms a diagonal with H8 but is in contact with Black\'s G8. Contact moves often help the opponent solidify their shape, so extending into open space is generally preferred unless blocking is necessary.\n\nReason:\nMove **I9** is selected because it capitalizes on the advantage given by Black\'s passive play at A1. By forming a diagonal "live 2" with the stone at H8, White expands into the open board area (bottom-right) and sets up multiple future connection possibilities (horizontal, vertical, and diagonal). It avoids immediate conflict with Black\'s G8, allowing White to dictate the flow of the game.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "I", "row": 9}'}
