ing and Planning for a Winning Strategy**\n\nPlaying I7, I make a 3. I see I can block a Black\'s potential diagonal with I8. And if Black blocks, I8 will form a vertical, Open 3! I must respond by blocking that line if Black tries to claim it.\n\n**Making the Final Decision and Executing the Play**\n\nLet\'s double check. If I play I7 and make that threat, and my opponent blocks me, I8 is my play! I have an Open 3. I must secure I8 to either block my opponent\'s claim to win or secure another White win!\n\nTherefore, the move is to play at **I7**. {"col": "I", "row": 7}\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The board shows a blocked horizontal line for White on row 9 (F9-I9), which cannot be used to win. However, White has a promising diagonal "live 2" consisting of H8 and G9. Black has disjointed stones but poses a potential threat at I8, which would connect H7 and J9 into an Open 3. White needs to seize the initiative to force Black into a defensive position.\n\nReason: Playing at I7 extends the H8-G9 diagonal to form an Open 3 (I7-H8-G9), which creates an immediate threat of forming a 4-in-a-row. Black will be forced to block this diagonal, likely at F10. This exchange is favorable for White because having a stone at I7 enables a powerful follow-up move at I8. Playing I8 later would not only block Black\'s potential diagonal threat (H7-J9) but also connect the White stones at I7 and I9 to form a new vertical Open 3, sustaining the attack.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "I", "row": 7}'}
